findings.description:
Entities have IDs and live in pre-allocated SoA arrays, but the document never says how removal works. The options are swap-with-last (indices change), a free-list with an alive mask (holes), or compaction. This choice affects:
- the behavior engine's subset index arrays, which go stale if indices move;
- client deltas, which need stable IDs and not array positions;
- the log and replay format;
- aggregation, where disaggregation spawns hundreds of individuals at once (line 104).

Line 84 says entity capacity sets pre-allocation size, but nothing says what happens when a spawn or disaggregation goes over capacity: reject, grow, or fail.

Step 4 handles "entity lifecycle events … queued during the tick", but nothing in v1 creates such events. There are no client commands, and the behavior engine is stateless and does not manage lifecycle. Either name who produces these events (predation kills? reproduction?) or remove the queue.

"Explicit lifecycle" promises "No silent failures, no undefined states", but the document never says what happens when:
- `engine.tick()` raises mid-tick;
- the engine returns NaN or Inf positions, which is likely with the Euler integration that 100-arch notes can drift at high force magnitudes;
- environment update fails;
- a client handler raises.

"Tick as transaction" implies the server can discard a failed tick and keep the previous state, but there is no rule for when to discard versus halt, and no rule for how many consecutive failures cause shutdown. The project's Fail Fast and no-silent-fallback rules mean this needs an explicit policy, such as "validate committed arrays for finite values; on failure log at ERROR, do not commit, halt after N consecutive failures". The server lifecycle states (starting, running, draining, stopped, faulted) are also never listed.

The Explicit lifecycle principle says the server knows how to "persist state on shutdown". Nothing else in the document defines persistence:
- there is no save format;
- there is no load-on-startup path (startup in line 57 always generates from config);
- there is no anticipated slice for it.

The slice plan lists "State Persistence and World Save/Load" as Future Work. Either remove the claim from the principle or make it a scoped deliverable. As written, the shutdown slice could be judged incomplete against this document.

Step 6 says to "Sleep for the remainder of the tick budget", and line 88 says to "continue at best effort" on overrun. The document never says whether simulated time per tick (dt) stays fixed when wall-clock time slips. This matters for:
- seasonal time scale (line 72: "advances with each tick based on a configurable time scale");
- replay fidelity;
- clients interpolating between frames.

Sleeping for the remainder also accumulates drift. The loop should schedule against absolute monotonic deadlines (`loop.time()`) and say whether it catches up or drops ticks after an overrun.

Line 112 calls it "the key architectural decision now" that the entity management layer support aggregation and variable-rate ticking from the start. The entity management description (line 70) contains nothing that does this:
- no entity kind distinction (individual vs aggregate);
- no per-entity tick-rate or last-evaluated-tick field for reduced-rate interpolation;
- no storage plan for aggregate descriptors (center of mass, heading, dispersion, count) next to the SoA arrays;
- no ID scheme that survives aggregate↔individual conversion.

Design Goals (line 35) similarly promise "region-aware entity management", but the term is never defined. The slice plan then defers all of it ("should use array layouts that do not preclude aggregation"), so no artifact actually owns this "key decision".

Fix: either name the two or three concrete fields and invariants the entity layer must carry now, or downgrade the claim to "not precluded" and hand it to 620.

The concept says v1 is "single-process world server, single browser client, handful of behavioral profiles", and the initiative plan says 620 "need not be designed proactively". Yet this section spends about 20 lines on:
- the 1M–4M GPU tier;
- multi-GPU dynamic partitioning;
- predictive partition rebalancing for merging 500K murmurations;
- comparisons to astrophysics N-body work and military wargaming.

None of this produces a requirement on any slice in this initiative except the aggregation hook above, and that hook is not concretely specified.

What to cut: move the 1M+ tiers, the "Group encounters at scale" paragraph and the "extreme scale ambitions" paragraph to the 620 architecture. Keep one paragraph that states the concrete v1 constraints: the 10K–50K operating range, the subset-evaluable tick, stable entity IDs, and the network-egress math that motivates slice 309.
